librqbit, vendored for Marquee

Upstream: https://github.com/ikatson/rqbit, tag v9.0.1,
commit a499d2f243d124e144aef137afe7cb304a6e3f36, directory crates/librqbit.
Copyright 2021 Igor Katson. Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0
(LICENSE is upstream's header, LICENSE-APACHE-2.0 the full text). Upstream
ships no NOTICE file of its own.

What was taken: src/, resources/, examples/, build.rs and README.md as in the
tag. Cargo.toml is the manifest published to crates.io for librqbit 9.0.1
(workspace inheritance resolved to concrete versions). The web UI (webui/) is
not vendored; it is only built with the `webui` feature, which Marquee never
enables. Sibling crates (librqbit-core, -dht, -peer-protocol, -utp, -upnp and
the rest) come unmodified from crates.io at 9.0.1, except librqbit-sha1-wrapper,
vendored beside this crate (see its NOTICE).

Marquee changes (each changed spot carries a "Marquee fork" comment):

- src/torrent_state/live/ext.rs (new): the external scheduler hook,
  `ExtSched` and `ExtEvent`, `TorrentStateLive::ext_request` and
  `ext_cancel`, `ManagedTorrent::ext_have_pieces`.
- src/torrent_state/live/mod.rs: event calls at the bitfield, have, choke,
  unchoke, extended handshake, peer death, block arrival and hash check
  sites; in drive mode the chunk requester only keeps the peer interested,
  blocks of one piece are accepted from any peer (first arrival wins,
  written blocks are never rewritten), and a choke forgets the peer's
  requests.
- src/torrent_state/live/peer/mod.rs: `cancel_inflight_request` and
  `forget_inflight_requests`.
- src/chunk_tracker.rs: `is_chunk_have`.
- src/session.rs, src/torrent_state/mod.rs, src/lib.rs: the `ext_sched`
  option and re-exports.
- src/torrent_state/streaming.rs: a `FileStream` no longer holds a blocking
  spawner permit for its lifetime (as many open streams as permits starved
  disk writes, and every stream then waited forever).
- Cargo.toml: an empty [workspace] table; the `rust-tls` feature turns on
  reqwest's `rustls-no-provider` (not its aws-lc `rustls`) and an optional
  `rustls` with ring.
- src/session.rs: the ring provider is installed before the first reqwest
  client is built (core backlog C11).
- src/session.rs, src/stream_connect.rs, src/lib.rs: `Destinations`, the
  address policy (plans/c27-torrent-policy.md). The connector refuses a
  peer outside it before dialing, and an incoming peer outside it is
  refused before its handshake is read (counted in `blocked_incoming`).
- src/session.rs, src/lib.rs: `Destinations::trackers`, the tracker policy
  handed to the UDP tracker client; with it the session's tracker HTTP
  client uses no proxy, follows no redirect, keeps no idle connection and
  resolves names through the policy. `tracker_comms` is re-exported. It
  gives up on a tracker after 10 s to connect and 30 s in all. A proxy
  cannot be combined with the policy (the session refuses to start), and a
  `.torrent` URL's literal host is checked against it.
- src/session.rs: `Destinations::dht`, the DHT's check, handed to both DHT
  constructors (persisted and not).
- src/bitv_factory.rs, src/session.rs: `FolderBitVFactory` and
  `SessionOptions::bitv_folder`, fast resume with no session store: each
  torrent's have-bitfield in `<folder>/<info hash>.have`, so a relaunch
  hashes a sample of its pieces instead of every file.
- src/bitv.rs: a disk-backed bitfield is also written at once when it is
  dropped (no fsync), for a process that ends right after.
- src/dht_utils.rs, src/session.rs: a magnet whose peers all failed says
  why the last one did ("input address stream exhausted" carries it).
- src/torrent_state/initializing.rs: the fast-resume sample counts a wrong
  hash as broken (upstream counted only read errors), and a broken bitfield
  is cleared by info hash.
- src/bitv.rs: a disk-backed bitfield syncs the data files of the pieces it
  newly claims before each write (the flusher's and the one on drop), so it
  never claims a piece the disk lost.
- src/bitv.rs, src/bitv_factory.rs, src/lib.rs, Cargo.toml: the data sync
  opens the file with write access (Windows flushes only such a handle);
  with the `sync-log` feature, data syncs and bitfield writes are logged in
  order for tests.
- src/torrent_state/initializing.rs: a full check's result is stored only
  after the data it claims is synced.
- src/torrent_state/live/mod.rs: a pause flushes the bitfield.
- src/torrent_state/live/evict.rs (new), src/chunk_tracker.rs,
  src/piece_tracker.rs, src/torrent_state/live/mod.rs, src/lib.rs: eviction
  rounds for a streaming window (plans/streaming-window.md 2.5): pieces are
  gated in memory, unclaimed on disk, punched with no lock and released,
  with `ExtEvent::PieceEvicted` sent inside the release lock.
- src/storage/mod.rs, src/storage/filesystem/{fs.rs,opened_file.rs,
  punch.rs (new),mod.rs}, Cargo.toml (libc): `TorrentStorage::punch`
  (forwarded through `Box`), APFS `F_PUNCHHOLE` and Linux or Android
  `fallocate` rounded inward to whole blocks, and `punch_supported`.
- src/bitv.rs: the flusher answers `flush_acked` after the fsync of a write
  that holds the snapshot, or with the step that failed; with `sync-log`,
  fsynced bitfields and punches are logged, and tests can hold a round, a
  stream read or a bitfield fsync, and make a step fail.
- src/torrent_state/streaming.rs: a stream takes its piece's read lock
  under the torrent read lock that saw the piece, and holds it over the
  read.
- src/torrent_state/initializing.rs: with no bitfield and files holding
  under an eighth of their selected length, nothing is claimed and no full
  check runs.
- src/torrent_state/live/evict.rs, src/torrent_state/live/mod.rs,
  src/chunk_tracker.rs: a round refuses with upload on or out of drive
  mode; a failed punch releases its pieces unclaimed; a round that stops
  early, and a pause that finds a gated piece, release it unclaimed;
  `PieceChecked(ok)`, the per-file count and `have_bytes` move under the
  lock that sets `have`.
- src/storage/filesystem/punch.rs: the punch is retried on EINTR.
- src/storage/filesystem/punch.rs: each `punch_supported` call probes on a
  scratch file of its own, so two probes at once never share one.
- src/torrent_state/live/mod.rs: a peer connection's death acts only on
  its own peer slot (upstream took a newer connection's state for its own
  when a peer disconnected as not needed was queued again before its old
  connection was gone, and could drop the peer for good).
- src/torrent_state/live/evict.rs, src/chunk_tracker.rs: clear_empty_pieces
  punches pieces with no chunk written, gated as orphans are (APFS fills
  holes between small extents at a sync after a punch).
- src/torrent_state/streaming.rs: ext_wake_streams, every waiting stream
  polls again (an owner that ended its playback fails them).
- src/torrent_state/live/mod.rs: with an external scheduler driving, the
  download limit holds the read of each received block (upstream applies it
  on the request path, which an external scheduler never takes).
- src/torrent_state/live/evict.rs, src/bitv.rs: the round's guard is armed
  before the first gate and releases what is still gated; a punch refused
  before any work keeps its pieces; a bitfield only in memory answers an
  acknowledged flush with a failure, so nothing is punched.
- src/storage/filesystem/opened_file.rs: 32-bit Android writes through the
  portable `pwrite_all` path (std's pwrite64). Its off_t is 32 bits, so
  nix's `pwritev` refused every offset past 2 GiB and the torrent stopped
  on the first chunk written there. That path is a function of its own,
  which the tests run on every target with a sparse write at 5 GiB.
- src/dht_utils.rs, src/session.rs: a resolved magnet lists the peer that
  sent its metadata first among the peers it met (upstream kept them in
  hash order), so the torrent's own add dials the one known to answer
  first.
- Cargo.toml: a [patch.crates-io] table with the sibling forks, so this
  crate's own tests build on their own as in the Marquee workspace.
